<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Máel Sechnaill mac Domnaill</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">948–1022</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Máel Sechnaill II, Malachy II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Máel Sechnaill mac Domnaill was King of Mide and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland">High King of Ireland</a> from 980 to 1002 and again from 1014 to 1022. He defeated the Norse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> at Tara in 980 and took Dublin, but in 1002 submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Boru</a>. After Brian's death at Clontarf in 1014 he resumed the high kingship, the last of the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> to hold it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mael-sechnaill-mac-domnaill.jpg" alt="Máel Sechnaill mac Domnaill" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Murphy · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_sculpture_of_M%C3%A1el_Seachnaill_II_in_Trim,_Co._Meath,_by_James_McKenna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Máel Sechnaill, also called Máel Sechnaill Mór, belonged to the Clann Cholmáin branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>. He was a grandson of Donnchad Donn, a great-grandson of Flann Sinna and a great-great-grandson of the first Máel Sechnaill, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mael-sechnaill-mac-maele-ruanaid">Máel Sechnaill mac Máele Ruanaid</a>. By his time the kingship of Tara alternated between Clann Cholmáin in the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenel-neogain">Cenél nEógain</a> in the north, a system that had survived the kings of Ulster, Munster and Leinster and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> invasions, and so he succeeded Domnall ua Néill in 980.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 980 Olaf Cuaran, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, attacked Meath with auxiliaries from the Scottish Isles and Mann, and was defeated at the Battle of Tara, where his heir Reginald was killed. Dublin surrendered after a siege of three days and nights, and according to the Annals of Tigernach Máel Sechnaill freed the slaves held there. At a meeting near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonfert">Clonfert</a> in 997 he made a truce with his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Boru</a>, King of Munster, leaving Brian the southern half of Ireland and himself the north and the high kingship; the two exchanged hostages.</p><p>When the Leinstermen and the Norse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> revolted against Brian late in 999, the two kings joined forces and, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a>, defeated them at Glenmama on 30 December 999, opening the road to Dublin. In 1002 Brian displaced him as high king, a bloodless change that came about because his northern kinsmen failed to support him. After Brian, his son and grandson died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-clontarf">Battle of Clontarf</a> in 1014, Máel Sechnaill regained the title with the aid of Flaithbertach Ua Néill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 2 September 1022. His two known wives were Gormflaith ingen Murchada and Máel Muire ingen Amlaíb, and his six known children included Conchobar, Flann, Congalach and Domnall. Brian's rise had ended the alternating succession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>, and Clann Cholmáin provided no further high kings, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenel-neogain">Cenél nEógain</a> later gave two, Domnall Ua Lochlainn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-mac-lochlainn">Muirchertach Mac Lochlainn</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Máel Sechnaill mac Domnaill” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Probably near Lyons Hill in County Kildare, the forces of Munster and Meath under Brian Boru and the High King <b>Máel Sechnaill II</b> crushed the armies of Leinster and Dublin led by King Máel Mórda, ending the brief Leinster revolt.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-30">30 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Mael Sechnaill mac Maele Ruanaid and <b>Mael Sechnaill mac Domnaill</b> were among the most powerful kings in Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mide">Kingdom of Mide</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ireland. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lough-ennell">Lough Ennell</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-7.92&amp;year=996" aria-label="Ireland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4374 329 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Máel Sechnaill mac Domnaill"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4824 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4824 599h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-7.92&amp;year=996">What was here in 996 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
